<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#SacredSong> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A song, chant, or liturgical vocal composition used in Afro-Atlantic ceremonial contexts. Includes oriki (Yorùbá praise poetry), canto de entrada (Lucumí invocation songs), chanson Vodou, and analogous forms. Songs may be classified by entity addressed, ceremony type, and liturgical language. Song text is among the most sensitive data in this module: song existence and entity association are community-restricted; text and melodic structure are initiated-only to elder-only."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#rhythmAlternateName> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "alternate rhythm name"@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#RhythmFamilyScheme> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#hasTopConcept> <https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#family-other> .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#inst-bata> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Two-headed conical hourglass drum of Yorùbá origin. In Lucumí tradition, the consecrated Añá bàtá set (iyá, itótele, okónkolo) is the highest-authority drum. Requires Olubatá/Aña initiation to play in sacred contexts."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko-ngoma> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/title> "Iroko Framework: Ngoma Module (Sacred Music, Rhythms, Songs, and Instruments)"@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#songTitle> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty> .
